1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,592
At ten past six on a Friday, Aziz stands in the mobile bank, ready for his MyID check.

2
00:00:05,642 --> 00:00:10,632
At the same minute, the MyID service starts to answer slowly, then not at all.

3
00:00:10,682 --> 00:00:12,504
Aziz looks at a spinner.

4
00:00:12,554 --> 00:00:17,192
What happens to him, and what happens to the platform?

5
00:00:17,242 --> 00:00:26,504
In Lesson 6, some configured values were timeouts: how long heeyID waits for MyID, TAF or the SMS service.

6
00:00:26,554 --> 00:00:32,288
This lesson shows the walls around every outside call: ports, adapters and breakers.

7
00:00:32,338 --> 00:00:34,160
Start in the middle.

8
00:00:34,210 --> 00:00:41,456
The domain of heeyID holds the rules: registration, proofing, linking, devices and login.

9
00:00:41,506 --> 00:00:44,720
The domain has no input and no output of its own.

10
00:00:44,770 --> 00:00:49,400
It never opens a connection, never reads a file, and never calls MyID.

11
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:52,232
When it needs something from outside, it calls a port.

12
00:00:52,282 --> 00:01:00,512
A port is an interface in the words of the domain: open a proofing session, send a code, find a customer by personal identification number.

13
00:01:00,562 --> 00:01:04,808
An adapter is the code behind the port that talks to one real service.

14
00:01:04,858 --> 00:01:17,432
heeyID has eight outgoing ports: MyID, TAF, the SMS service, the push service, the customer lookup, the antifraud system, the staff identity and the security monitoring of the bank.

15
00:01:17,482 --> 00:01:22,112
Each port has two implementations: the real adapter, and a simulator.

16
00:01:22,162 --> 00:01:30,344
The simulator plays the service, with every answer that the contract allows: success, each failure reason, a timeout and a down mode.

17
00:01:30,394 --> 00:01:34,640
The acceptance tests run against the simulators, with no internet.

18
00:01:34,690 --> 00:01:38,216
A nightly run uses the MyID test environment.

19
00:01:38,266 --> 00:01:42,584
I think the simulator is the part that most teams skip, and then regret.

20
00:01:42,634 --> 00:01:45,152
Without it, you test only the good days.

21
00:01:45,202 --> 00:01:47,024
Now the walls.

22
00:01:47,074 --> 00:01:51,248
Each outgoing call has a timeout, and the timeout is a policy.

23
00:01:51,298 --> 00:01:53,384
Each adapter also has a breaker.

24
00:01:53,434 --> 00:02:01,520
After a configured number of failures in a row, the breaker opens: the dependency is down, and calls fail at once, with no waiting.

25
00:02:01,570 --> 00:02:07,448
A probe checks the service at a configured interval, and closes the breaker when the service answers again.

26
00:02:07,498 --> 00:02:12,152
While the breaker is open, an alert tells operations that a dependency is down.

27
00:02:12,202 --> 00:02:17,696
And now the most important rule: each dependency has one fixed behaviour when it is down.

28
00:02:17,746 --> 00:02:21,752
The adapter makes that behaviour impossible to bypass.

29
00:02:21,802 --> 00:02:28,088
A timeout always gives a named outcome, never an exception that each caller can read in its own way.

30
00:02:28,138 --> 00:02:29,960
Here are the fixed behaviours.

31
00:02:30,010 --> 00:02:33,608
MyID is down: the customer stays at the current level.

32
00:02:33,658 --> 00:02:38,984
The attempt does not count against the limits, and no proofing happens later in the background.

33
00:02:39,034 --> 00:02:45,440
TAF is down: the codes go through the SMS service of the bank, and the attempt is not scored.

34
00:02:45,490 --> 00:02:52,664
There is one exception: when the state services portal refuses this bank as a trusted bank, there is no fallback.

35
00:02:52,714 --> 00:02:59,264
The SMS service is down: no code can go, and the customer sees that the service is not available.

36
00:02:59,314 --> 00:03:02,888
Notifications wait in Kafka, and go again later.

37
00:03:02,938 --> 00:03:10,448
The push service is down: the approval request waits for the app to ask for it, and notifications go by SMS only.

38
00:03:10,498 --> 00:03:18,824
The customer lookup is down: the link waits as pending, a worker tries again during the configured period, and the step-up still completes.

39
00:03:18,874 --> 00:03:25,640
The antifraud system is down: its events wait in Kafka, and no journey waits for antifraud.

40
00:03:25,690 --> 00:03:27,896
Back to Aziz and his spinner.

41
00:03:27,946 --> 00:03:34,328
The MyID adapter waits the configured time, then gives a named outcome: the provider is not available.

42
00:03:34,378 --> 00:03:38,000
Aziz sees a plain message, and he stays at level one.

43
00:03:38,050 --> 00:03:39,872
His attempt does not count.

44
00:03:39,922 --> 00:03:46,808
The failures continue, so the breaker opens, and the next customers get the same answer at once, with no spinner.

45
00:03:46,858 --> 00:03:49,136
The panel shows the status of the provider.

46
00:03:49,186 --> 00:03:54,512
When MyID comes back, the probe closes the breaker, and Aziz tries again.

47
00:03:54,562 --> 00:03:56,864
Two more rules keep adapters safe.

48
00:03:56,914 --> 00:04:01,592
First, no adapter writes a request body or a response body to a log.

49
00:04:01,642 --> 00:04:05,552
It writes the reference, the outcome and the time that the call took.

50
00:04:05,602 --> 00:04:12,416
Second, hosts, ports and credentials come from the installation configuration and the key store, never from the code.

51
00:04:12,466 --> 00:04:21,224
The MyID adapter and the push adapter even run as their own services, so only they hold the MyID credential and the push credential.

52
00:04:21,274 --> 00:04:25,016
To recap: a domain with no input and no output.

53
00:04:25,066 --> 00:04:29,456
A port for each outside service, with a real adapter and a simulator.

54
00:04:29,506 --> 00:04:34,424
A timeout and a breaker on every call, and one fixed behaviour for each outage.

55
00:04:34,474 --> 00:04:37,736
So Aziz waits a few seconds, not forever.

56
00:04:37,786 --> 00:04:45,056
But each adapter holds a secret: the MyID credential, the TAF client secret, the push credential.

57
00:04:45,106 --> 00:04:47,984
And the platform keeps passports encrypted.

58
00:04:48,034 --> 00:04:49,544
Where do all those keys live?

59
00:04:49,594 --> 00:04:51,416
Next: keys.
